writing:{
 title:'Task 1 – Bar chart (20 phút)',
 prompt:'The chart below shows the types of housing that families lived in in a Vietnamese city in 1995, 2010 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.',
 chart:{type:'bar',title:'Types of housing in a city (% of families)',unit:'%',series:['1995','2010','2025'],cats:['Tube house','Apartment','Rented room','Villa'],data:[[60,45,30],[15,30,45],[20,20,17],[5,5,8]]},
 min:150,max:200,
 wex:[
  ['Overview nào tốt nhất?',['Overall, apartments rose from 15% to 45%.','Overall, tube houses became less common while apartments became much more popular, overtaking tube houses by 2025. Rented rooms and villas changed little.','Overall, people like apartments.'],1,'Overview: 2 xu hướng chính + nhóm ít thay đổi, không số liệu chi tiết.'],
  ['Câu nào mô tả đúng nhà ống?',['The proportion of families living in tube houses fell steadily from 60% to 30%.','Tube houses increased to 60%.','Tube houses were stable.'],0,'60 → 45 → 30: fell steadily / halved.'],
  ['“Apartments ______ tube houses as the most common type of housing in 2025.”',['overtook','overtake','were overtaken'],0,'overtake (quá khứ: overtook) = vượt qua.'],
  ['Cách chia đoạn thân bài hợp lý nhất:',['Đoạn 1: 1995; đoạn 2: 2010; đoạn 3: 2025','Đoạn 1: nhà ống & căn hộ (thay đổi lớn); đoạn 2: phòng thuê & biệt thự (ít thay đổi)','Mỗi loại nhà một đoạn'],1,'Nhóm theo xu hướng giúp so sánh dễ hơn.'],
  ['Câu nào dùng số liệu đúng cách?',['Rented rooms remained fairly stable, at around a fifth of families, before dipping slightly to 17% in 2025.','Rented rooms were 20, 20, 17.','Rented rooms are the most popular.'],0,'Số liệu gắn vào câu, có từ chỉ mức độ.'],
  ['Thì động từ chính cho biểu đồ này:',['quá khứ cho 1995, 2010; quá khứ/hiện tại tùy ngữ cảnh cho 2025','chỉ hiện tại đơn','tương lai'],0,'1995–2025 đã qua: chủ yếu dùng quá khứ đơn.']],
 outline:['Mở bài: The bar chart illustrates the proportions of families living in four types of housing in a Vietnamese city in 1995, 2010 and 2025.','Overview: nhà ống giảm, căn hộ tăng mạnh và vượt nhà ống năm 2025; phòng thuê và biệt thự ít thay đổi.','Thân bài 1: tube houses 60 → 45 → 30 (halved); apartments 15 → 30 → 45 (tripled), overtook.','Thân bài 2: rented rooms ~20%, dipped to 17%; villas smallest, 5% → 8%.'],
 model55:'The bar chart shows the types of housing that families in a city lived in in 1995, 2010 and 2025.<br><br>Overall, tube houses went down and apartments went up. Rented rooms and villas did not change much.<br><br>In 1995, 60% of families lived in tube houses. This went down to 45% in 2010 and 30% in 2025. Apartments were only 15% in 1995, but they went up to 30% in 2010 and 45% in 2025. In 2025, apartments were the most popular.<br><br>About 20% of families lived in rented rooms in 1995 and 2010, and it was 17% in 2025. Villas were the smallest group. They were 5% in 1995 and 2010 and 8% in 2025.',
 model:'The bar chart illustrates the proportions of families living in four types of housing in a Vietnamese city in 1995, 2010 and 2025.<br><br><i>Overall</i>, tube houses <b>became considerably less common</b>, while apartments <b>grew rapidly in popularity</b> and had <b>overtaken</b> tube houses by 2025. <i>By contrast</i>, the figures for rented rooms and villas changed only slightly.<br><br>In 1995, tube houses were by far the most common type of home, accommodating 60% of families. <i>However</i>, this figure <b>fell steadily</b> to 45% in 2010 and then to 30% in 2025, <b>halving</b> over the period. Apartments followed the opposite trend: <b>only 15%</b> of families lived in them in 1995, but the proportion doubled to 30% in 2010 and <b>tripled</b> to 45% by 2025, making apartments the most popular choice.<br><br>Rented rooms remained <b>fairly stable</b> at around a fifth of families in the first two years, <i>before</i> dipping slightly to 17% in 2025. Villas were the least common type of housing throughout, <b>accounting for</b> just 5% in both 1995 and 2010 and rising marginally to 8% in the final year.',
 note:'Khoảng 175 từ, band 6.0–6.5. Viết trong 20 phút: đọc đề và dàn ý 3 phút, viết 14 phút, soát 3 phút. Chia thân bài theo xu hướng (thay đổi lớn / ít thay đổi).',
 checklist:['Mở bài paraphrase','Overview có 2 xu hướng chính, không số liệu','Thân bài nhóm theo xu hướng','Mỗi số liệu đi kèm động từ/từ chỉ mức độ','Thì quá khứ đúng','Viết xong trong 20 phút']
},
speaking:{
 task:'Ghi âm Part 2 (2 phút) và câu Part 3 số 1, gửi cô qua Zalo.',
 items:[
 ['Do you live in a house or an apartment?',"I live in a tube house with my parents and my younger brother. It's quite narrow, but it has four floors, so each of us has enough space."],
 ['What is your favourite room in your home?',"Probably my bedroom, because it's quiet and it has a big window. I do most of my homework there, and in the evening I can see the lights of the city."],
 ['Would you like to move to a different home in the future?',"Yes, I'd like to live in an apartment near the city centre when I go to university. It would be more convenient, and I'd save a lot of time travelling."],
 ['Do you prefer old buildings or modern buildings?',"I prefer old buildings, like the ones in Hoi An, because they have character and history. Modern buildings are comfortable, but many of them look the same."]],
 part2:{cue:'Describe a building that you think is interesting.',bullets:['where it is','what it looks like','what it is used for','and explain why you find it interesting.'],
  notes:'Ho Chi Minh City Post Office · District 1, near the cathedral · French style, yellow walls, big arched roof, map on wall · post office + souvenirs · history, still in use, tourists',
  model:"The building I'd like to describe is the Central Post Office in Ho Chi Minh City. It's in District 1, right next to the Notre-Dame Cathedral, so it's very easy to find. It was built more than a hundred years ago, when Vietnam was a French colony, and you can see the French style clearly. From the outside, it has bright yellow walls, tall windows and a big clock above the entrance. Inside, the ceiling is very high and curved, and there are two large old maps painted on the walls. What I find interesting is that it's not just a museum. It's still a working post office, so people send letters and parcels there every day, and there are also small shops selling postcards and souvenirs for tourists. I went there with my cousin last year, and we sent a postcard to ourselves for fun. I find the building interesting because it shows how the city has changed. If you stand outside, you can see modern high-rise buildings in the distance, but inside the post office it feels as if time has stopped. I think it's a great example of how old buildings can still be useful today."},
 part3:[
 ['Why do many people in cities live in apartments?',"Mainly because land in city centres is very expensive, so apartments are more affordable. They also often have facilities like security, parking and swimming pools, which families find convenient."],
 ['Should old buildings be protected or replaced with new ones?',"I think historic buildings should be protected, because they are part of a city's identity and they attract tourists. However, buildings that are unsafe or have no special value can be replaced, especially if the city needs more housing."],
 ['What will homes be like in the future?',"I expect homes will be smaller but smarter. Many devices will be controlled by phones, and buildings will probably use solar panels and better insulation to save energy. If cities keep growing, more people will live in high-rise buildings."]]
},
